---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const path = '/use-cases/abandoned-cart';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/abandoned-cart');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/abandoned-cart');
const candidates = ['sequenzy', 'klaviyo', 'omnisend', 'postscript', 'drip', 'recart', 'firepush', 'yotpo-email-sms', 'privy', 'rejoiner'].map(s => appBySlug(s)!).filter(Boolean);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a><article class="prose-shopify card mt-6">
<h1>Shopify SMS cart recovery: cancel the reminder when intent changes</h1>
<p>For a retention operator, cart recovery is a state-management problem before it is a copywriting problem. Adding an item, starting checkout and failing payment are different events. A phone number attached to an order is not evidence of permission for promotional reminders. Define the event and channel eligibility before choosing a vendor.</p>
<h2>Separate cart, checkout and payment failure</h2><p>Record the source event, customer identifier, cart or checkout identifier, variant IDs, currency, last activity and permission reference. Anonymous browsing cannot be treated as an addressable SMS audience. Shopify's <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/abandoned-checkouts">abandoned checkout documentation</a> describes recovery email coverage for Online Store and Buy Button, and payment-event details that can help investigate failed checkout. That is not a promise that a third-party SMS integration has identical coverage.</p>
<h2>Define the queued-send contract</h2><p>Enroll only after the chosen inactivity condition is met. Before release, recheck completed orders, opt-outs, inventory, recipient timing eligibility and competing recovery flows. Use a stable checkout identifier to avoid repeating enrollment when an event is retried. Decide whether a new cart replaces an old reminder or starts a separate journey.</p><p>If email, Shopify recovery and an SMS app can all send, assign an owner for the whole journey. Explicitly cancel or suppress the competing recovery path when purchase completes. Do not use an unopened email alone as permission to send a text.</p>
<h2>Three proposed cart cases</h2><ul><li><strong>Furniture:</strong> a customer may need delivery dimensions or financing details rather than a coupon. Put the explanation on a landing page or email; reserve a text for a permitted, useful reminder.</li><li><strong>Subscription starter:</strong> show billing cadence and skip/cancel terms accurately. Check that the recovery link preserves the selling plan and does not stack an unintended introductory offer.</li><li><strong>Limited apparel:</strong> if the selected size sells out while waiting, stop the recovery message. Offer a separate <a href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">restock signup</a> where appropriate; do not silently substitute a size.</li></ul>
<h2>Repair broken links before optimizing timing</h2><p>Ask the integration owner to demonstrate a valid recovery link and its missing-data fallback. Shopify's <a href="https://shopify.dev/changelog/posts/abandonedcheckouturl-now-requires-level-2-protected-customer-data-access">protected customer data changelog</a> documents access requirements for abandoned checkout URLs and warns that missing access can remove the field. Review the applicable rollout and app status with the supplier. A development-store success alone does not establish production access.</p>
<h2>Make incentives a controlled branch</h2><p>Start the proposed message with the product and a useful route back. A discount is an experiment with a cost, not a mandatory second message. Have finance set the margin constraint for the actual basket, including shipping support, returns and stacked codes. Existing full-price buyers and first-time price-sensitive shoppers may warrant different hypotheses; historical code use alone does not prove a customer requires an offer.</p><p>See <a href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">discount strategy</a> for offer design and <a href="/use-cases/browse-abandonment">browse abandonment</a> for lower-intent visitors. Cart addition does not itself establish checkout intent.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance checks and measurement</h2><p>Queue a controlled reminder, then complete the order, withdraw permission, remove stock and replay the source event in separate cases. Expected outcomes are cancellation, suppression, no unavailable-item promise and no duplicate. These checks are proposed; this page reports no merchant test results.</p><p>Track eligible checkouts, queued and canceled sends, delivered messages, fees, opt-outs and orders by stable ID. Report a recovery rate with a defined denominator. Use a randomized eligible holdout where feasible to estimate incremental contribution rather than claiming all attributed orders. Keep timing and offer changes separate so the result has an interpretable cause.</p>
<h2>App discovery and demonstration brief</h2><p>The catalog below preserves routes for comparison; it is not a ranking or confirmation that each app supports this workflow or native SMS. For this use case, ask each supplier to demonstrate the state transitions described above, the required integration and plan, export fields, failure handling and the full messaging quote. Include Sequenzy in that evaluation only where its documented channel and integration scope meets your requirements.</p><ul>{candidates.map(app => <li><a href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></li>)}</ul><p>For permission and recipient timing design, use the <a href="/guides/consent">consent</a> and <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours</a> guides. This workflow plan does not establish legal applicability or guarantee delivery.</p></article></section></BaseLayout>
